#: manual/windows.md:1
msgid "Windows"
msgstr ""

#: manual/cost.md:51
msgid "**Game.** Every floating window is under one canvas, and Unity rebuilds a canvas as a whole. Any change in any window, an item moving or a slot lighting up under the mouse, re-batches every graphic of every open window: O(g · W) per change."
msgstr ""

#: manual/cost.md:53
msgid "**Fix.** Each container window gets its own nested canvas, so a change re-batches only its own window: O(g) per change. The order of growth per window is the same; the factor W, the other open windows, goes away."
msgstr ""

#: manual/cost.md:55
msgid "**Result.** Identical: same draw order, pixels-per-unit and shader channels, and clicks still reach the window."
msgstr ""

#: manual/cost.md:59
msgid "**Game.** Opening a window moves it from its home under the floating-window canvas, and closing it moves it back. Each move makes every graphic in the window re-register: O(g) per open and per close."
msgstr ""

#: manual/cost.md:61
msgid "**Fix.** A closed window stays where it is. The first open still costs O(g); after that, opening only brings it to the front, a constant amount of work."
msgstr ""

#: manual/cost.md:63
msgid "**Measured.** About 140 ms of the 280 ms stutter of the storage-preset hotkey, which opens four full windows at once."
msgstr ""

#: manual/cost.md:65
msgid "**Result.** Identical. A window is kept only within its own scene and goes home before it is destroyed."
msgstr ""

#: manual/cost.md:69
msgid "**Game.** Hiding a window deactivates it, and showing it activates it again: every component in it is switched off and on, O(g) each way."
msgstr ""

#: manual/cost.md:71
msgid "**Fix.** The window's own canvas and raycaster are switched off instead: constant work per hide and per show. The first open of each window still costs O(g)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/cost.md:73
msgid "**Measured.** About 110 ms each way for one large storage window before; nearly instant after its first open."
msgstr ""

#: manual/cost.md:75
msgid "**Result.** Identical: the game's own hide steps run in the same order, and the window answers \"not visible\" as before."
msgstr ""

#: manual/cost.md:79
msgid "**Game.** The storage-preset hotkey opens or closes all W windows of a preset in one frame: O(g · W) in that frame."
msgstr ""

#: manual/cost.md:81
msgid "**Fix.** The same windows open or close one per frame, the first at once. **This fix does not reduce the work**: the total stays O(g · W). It spreads it over W frames, so no single frame does more than O(g) and animations keep moving."
msgstr ""

#: manual/cost.md:83
msgid "**Result.** Identical: each window's tab selection is applied right after its own opening, and pressing the hotkey again finishes any pending windows first."
msgstr ""

#: manual/cost.md:85
#: manual/index.md:21
#: manual/index.md:22
#: manual/index.md:23
#: manual/index.md:24
#: manual/index.md:25
#: manual/inventories.md:1
msgid "Inventories"
msgstr ""

#: manual/cost.md:89
msgid "**Game.** Every item that enters or leaves an inventory, and every tag change on an item, lays the item out and then lays out its whole inventory again: O(n) per change. Filling an inventory of n items costs n inventory layouts of growing size, O(n²); a tag change on one item in a full storage bay lays out the whole bay."
msgstr ""

#: manual/cost.md:91
msgid "**Fix.** The inventory layouts asked for while an item is laid out are collected, and each inventory is laid out once, just before the frame is drawn: O(n) per inventory per frame however many changes it had. The item's own layout and everything else still run at once."
msgstr ""

#: manual/cost.md:93
msgid "**Measured.** One save load ran 161,804 item layouts and 7,000 inventory layouts in a single frame, about 1.8 s."
msgstr ""

#: manual/cost.md:95
msgid "**Result.** Identical on screen, because the layout happens before the frame is drawn. This is the one fix that moves work to a later point in the frame rather than combining it in the same call, because in the game's design the inventory layout is triggered from inside every item change and cannot be combined there."
msgstr ""

#: manual/cost.md:99
msgid "**Game.** A save loads in parts, one per inventory. While a part loads, every tag change and every move of an item recomputes its shape and state and then works out its UI again, about t times per item. Working out an item's UI also works out the UI of every container around it, and every inventory layout works out the UI of all its items again. With a container nesting depth of d, an item's UI is worked out O(t · d) times, the same inputs nearly every time but the last."
msgstr ""

#: manual/cost.md:101
msgid "**Fix.** The UI work of one part is combined into a single pass at the end of that same part: each item whose UI was asked for is worked out once, then each container around them once. That is O(N + P) per part. Shapes, states, placement, inventory layouts and every load step still run exactly as often as in the game, so their own cost, O(N · t) shape computations, is unchanged."
msgstr ""

#: manual/cost.md:103
msgid "**Measured.** On a store of 1,342 items: 61,551 UI requests became 1,343 runs (11 more were for items destroyed during the load, and skipped). With the long-frame profiler on, the time spent working out item UI fell from 576 ms to 57 ms. Without the profiler, and with the other fixes on, the save load took 2,718 ms and 2,761 ms with the option off and on, a difference within the spread between loads: once the other fixes have made each UI walk cheap, removing the repeated ones no longer shows. An earlier comparison of 3,645 ms against 2,903 ms was made with the profiler on, which made every walk slower, and overstated the gain."
msgstr ""

#: manual/cost.md:105
msgid "**Result.** Identical. An item's UI is worked out from its current shape, state and contents, so working it out once at the end gives what the game's last time gives. Loading the same save with the option off and on put the same number of items in every inventory."
msgstr ""

#: manual/cost.md:109
msgid "**Game.** Each item's UI has k = 13 parts, and windows, inventories and machine panels are built the same way. All but the first part of each element are created in a shared temporary place and then each is moved to where it belongs. Creating is unavoidable; moving costs a constant per move, about 73 µs for a bare part inside the game's interface and about 28 µs outside it, whatever the part holds. That is k − 1 moves per element, O(E · k) moves for E elements."
msgstr ""

#: manual/cost.md:111
msgid "**Fix.** In 33 builders, 132 kinds of part are created directly where they belong, so their moves are gone. The creations remain, so **the order of growth is still O(E · k)**; what goes away is one of the two per-part steps, and the more expensive one. Moves the game makes for other reasons, such as laying out an inventory's background, are not touched: about 8,000 of them remain while the 1,342-item store loads."
msgstr ""

#: manual/cost.md:113
msgid "**Measured.** With items alone, creating the UI of 1,342 items fell from 2,040 ms to 363 ms and the whole save load from 4,655 ms to 2,833 ms. Adding windows, inventories and machine panels raised the parts created in place from 16,236 to 20,053 per load and took about 0.1 s to 0.25 s more off it."
msgstr ""

#: manual/cost.md:115
msgid "**Result.** Identical: each part ends with the same parent, the same place among its siblings and the same position, rotation and scale, and it starts up the same way."
msgstr ""

#: manual/cost.md:119
msgid "**Game.** Every tag change and every move works out the item's shape and state, t times per item, O(N · t) in all: 58,148 times for the 1,342-item store. Each time, the game searches everything under the item for inventories, and for that builds c = 4 collections and walks them, even for an item with nothing under it."
msgstr ""

#: manual/cost.md:121
msgid "**Fix.** For an item with neither an inventory window nor an examine window the search returns the same empty answer at once, with nothing built. **The number of shape checks is unchanged, O(N · t)**, and so is the rest of each one, including the copy of the item's tags; only the constant of the search goes away for such items. Items with anything under them are searched as before."
msgstr ""

#: manual/cost.md:123
msgid "**Measured.** 56,211 of the 58,148 searches were skipped. With the load timed method by method, the shape computation's own time fell from 118 ms to 85 ms to 89 ms; part of both is the timing itself."
msgstr ""

#: manual/cost.md:125
msgid "**Result.** Identical: the empty answer is the one the search gives for an item with nothing under it, and the caller only reads it."
msgstr ""

#: manual/cost.md:129
msgid "**Game.** While items are selected, and while they are dragged, the game every frame switches off all s lit highlight squares and lights them again, so each one rebuilds and its canvas is re-batched. To find each item's slot it searches a fresh copy of the inventory's item list: O(m · n) per frame. Over the items' own container, a group drag also checks every selected item against every item in the inventory, and for each of those searches the list of selected items: O(m² · n) per frame."
msgstr ""

#: manual/cost.md:131
msgid "**Fix.** The same squares are lit, but only squares whose state changes are touched, and each item's slot is kept while the item stays put, so no lists are copied. The fit check itself is not made faster: **it still costs O(m² · n) when it runs**, but it runs again only when the drop position, the selection or an inventory's contents change, instead of every frame. Every other condition of the check still runs every frame."
msgstr ""

#: manual/cost.md:133
msgid "**Measured.** Not timed. Holding and dragging a large selection no longer stutters, inside the original container as well."
msgstr ""

#: manual/cost.md:135
msgid "**Result.** Identical: the squares end every frame as the game leaves them, and the drop itself still runs the game's own check."
msgstr ""

