#: manual/cost.md:153
msgid "**Game.** Before the save loads, the game reads the store's whole entry, S bytes, into a new store object to look at u of its upgrades, and drops it; the real load then reads the whole entry again. Both reads build every string and object in S, mostly the JSON of every inventory: 2 · O(S) in time and in what is built."
msgstr ""

#: manual/cost.md:155
msgid "**Fix.** The early look passes over everything but the upgrades. Passing over still reads every character of S, so **the order of growth stays O(S)**; what goes away is building the strings and objects of the rest, O(S) of allocation, leaving O(u). The real load is unchanged."
msgstr ""

#: manual/cost.md:157
msgid "**Measured.** With the load timed method by method, the early look fell from 131 ms to 147 ms down to 79 ms to 81 ms."
msgstr ""

#: manual/cost.md:159
msgid "**Result.** Identical: the upgrades are read by Easy Save's own reader exactly as before, and nothing else of the early copy is read by the game."
msgstr ""

#: manual/cost.md:163
msgid "**Game.** Entering the store writes K = 57 settings and key bindings one at a time. Each write reads the settings file, F bytes, merges in its one value, writes a temporary file and swaps it in: O(K · F) read and O(K · F) written, and K file swaps, which cost far more than the bytes. Nearly every value is the one already in the file."
msgstr ""

#: manual/cost.md:165
msgid "**Fix.** Each value is first read back: still K reads, O(K · F), but **only a setting that changed is written**, so in the usual case nothing is written and nothing is swapped."
msgstr ""

#: manual/cost.md:167
msgid "**Measured.** With the load timed method by method, the settings step fell from 496 ms to 10 ms: 0 written, 57 left out. The time from the end of the save load to the first frame fell from about 540 ms to about 155 ms."
msgstr ""

#: manual/cost.md:169
msgid "**Result.** Identical: a write is left out only where the file already holds the same key with the same value, so the file ends the same."
msgstr ""

#: manual/cost.md:173
msgid "**Game.** After leaving the store, R references keep pointing at it, and any one of them keeps the whole store, all its items, inventories and windows, in memory until the next store replaces each reference. Each later load in the same session starts with that memory still in use."
msgstr ""

#: manual/cost.md:175
msgid "**Fix.** When another scene replaces the store, the R references are let go, O(R), and memory is collected once while the menu is idle. **This fix does not make a load faster by itself**: it stops the memory of each store left from staying in use until the next one."
msgstr ""

#: manual/cost.md:177
msgid "**Measured.** In the store, 237 MB of managed memory were in use; after leaving it and a full collection, 158 MB, with one left-behind holder found, a list of three destroyed cameras in the game's own letterbox manager."
msgstr ""

#: manual/cost.md:179
msgid "**Result.** Identical: each reference is cleared only where the game no longer reads it."
msgstr ""

#: manual/cost.md:181
#: manual/customers.md:1
#: manual/index.md:29
msgid "Customers"
msgstr ""

#: manual/cost.md:185
msgid "**Game.** To decide whether a customer buys a container, the game lists every item anywhere inside it and runs the full check on each one, and each of those lists and checks everything inside itself again. An item k levels below the container is reached once for every combination of the containers between them, so it can be checked up to 2^(k−1) times, and every listing builds a new list. For the shallow nesting of most stores this looks roughly quadratic; it grows much faster with depth."
msgstr ""

#: manual/cost.md:187
msgid "**Fix.** The same rule is evaluated bottom-up once per item per call: O(N) for N items inside, plus one walk over the contents. Answers are kept only within that one call. Contents nested more than 32 levels deep in the game's item tree get no answer from this rule, and the game's own check runs for that call instead."
msgstr ""

#: manual/cost.md:189
msgid "**Measured.** Not timed separately. The rule is checked outside the game against a plain version of the game's rule on 2,000 random stores, with the same answer every time (see [Building](building.md))."
msgstr ""

#: manual/cost.md:191
msgid "**Result.** Identical: the same answers for the current state."
msgstr ""

#: manual/customers.md:5
msgid "**Problem.** When the game decides whether the customer will buy an item (`StoreClient.IsClientBuyingThisItem`), it also checks a container's contents. It lists every item anywhere inside, runs the full check on each one, and each of those lists and checks its own contents again. An item k levels below the container can be checked up to 2^(k−1) times, and every level allocates a new list; for shallow storage that looks roughly quadratic, and it grows much faster with depth."
msgstr ""

#: manual/customers.md:7
msgid "The game runs this check when an item is placed on the counter, when it decides whether something can be sold, and in the negotiation screen, once for every item in the back inventory."
msgstr ""

#: manual/customers.md:9
msgid "**Fix.** The rule, read from the game's code:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/customers.md:11
msgid "an item is **bought** when it is acceptable on its own (the game's own check with `ignoreContents`) and, if its contents are checked, everything inside it is bought;"
msgstr ""

#: manual/customers.md:12
msgid "contents are **checked** unless the item is currently locked, has no content window, or is an advanced gun."
msgstr ""

#: manual/customers.md:14
msgid "`IsClientBuyingThisItem` and `IsClientRefusingItem`, in the forms that include contents, are patched to evaluate exactly that rule bottom-up, once per item per call. Answers are reused only within that call, never across calls or frames, so the result is always the game's own for the current state. The single-item form runs unchanged."
msgstr ""

#: manual/customers.md:16
msgid "Contents nested more than 32 levels deep in the game's item tree get no answer from the rule. A container's windows count as levels too. For such a call the game's own check runs instead, and its checks of the items inside come back through the fix one level down each time, so even then the answer is the game's."
msgstr ""

#: manual/customers.md:18
msgid "**When it starts.** The game's own check uses a list of localized water names that the game fills in the first time it is needed. Installing the fix fills it in too, and mods start before the game applies the player's language. So the fix waits until the language the game chose at startup is applied, and only then installs. If that has not happened within five minutes, the fix stays off for the session and `Player.log` says so. Without this wait, water names would stay in English for the session and water would stop selling in any other language."
msgstr ""

